{"cluster_id": "C-1562", "topic": "dropRefBestEffort swallows rollback failure with no logging", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-035", "B008-025"], "canonical_claim": "dropRefBestEffort swallows rollback failure with no logging: a correlated backend outage during partial-commit rollback can leave a permanently-live phantom ref, since GC only reclaims unreferenced objects and won't clean it up. Proposed fix: add tryLogCurrentException (CachedPartFolderAccess.cpp).; `CachedPartFolderAccess::dropRefBestEffort` is `noexcept`, swallows cleanup failures, and relies on garbage collection to reclaim lingering debris.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/review1.md#minor-issues", "docs/superpowers/plans/2026-07-08-cas-part-folder-cache-phase2.md#task-4"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1563", "topic": "`ObjectStorageBackend", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M-073"], "canonical_claim": "`ObjectStorageBackend::list` in `EmulatedSingleProcess` mode fills `lk.token` from `child->metadata->etag` (`CasObjectStorageBackend.cpp:781-783`) while emulated `head`/`get`/`putOverwrite`/`deleteExact` all use `emuObserveToken`; a consumer that reuses `listed.token` for `deleteExact` (the orphan-manifest sweep, `CasOrphanManifestSweep.cpp:300`) can get a token mismatch and leave GC debris undeleted in that test-only mode. Production `Native` S3 is unaffected because both `list` and `head` return the real S3 ETag.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-06-scenario-validation-night.md#notes"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1564", "topic": "GC registered defect R2", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-068"], "canonical_claim": "GC registered defect R2: `PartWriteTxn`'s destructor unconditionally retired its build sequence, advancing the GC floor while the precommit grant's outcome was still uncertain, making an uncertain owner grant look dead; the fix is a duty queue with the failure direction inverted (settle then retire then pop), where any throw retains the duty and a full queue pins the floor, since an advanced floor is an irreversible authorization while a pinned one only costs latency.", "issue_ids": ["R2"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/how-we-got-here.md#turn-stage-b"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1565", "topic": "rev", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-117"], "canonical_claim": "The rev.8 disk-lifecycle whole-increment final review found two Important pre-release defects, both TOCTOU races around the `gc_scheduler_mutex`: (I-1) a manual `SYSTEM CONTENT ADDRESSED GC RUN` admitted while the pool was `Live` could queue behind FORGET's `gc_scheduler_mutex` and, once granted, resume and run a full GC round against a since-decommissioned (`Vanished`) pool; (I-2) `runGcRebuildNow` did not take `gc_scheduler_mutex` at all, making a GC rebuild invisible to FORGET and able to durably write after FORGET believed all GC threads were stopped. Both were fixed with a lock-then-gate pattern (matching `gcStart`) plus RED-demo regression tests.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-22-unattended-rev7-lifecycle.md#final-review-verdict"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1566", "topic": "post-soak leak-rca investigation verdict", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-135"], "canonical_claim": "A post-soak leak-rca investigation verdict: the T8v2 soak's 169,837 vs 79 reachable/unreachable checkpoint reading was NOT a leak but an AwaitingGc fold backlog mid-churn that drained to 14 by run end; the real bug was the tripwire formula, fixed by moving the strict check to final-converge (mid-run track-only).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-21-unattended-reftablestate-experiments.md#timeline"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1567", "topic": "Opt-round \u00a74 review Minor 2 (pre-existing, not introduced by the round)", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-139"], "canonical_claim": "Opt-round \u00a74 review Minor 2 (pre-existing, not introduced by the round): `CasBlobAdoptTrusted` increment and the manifest-trust audit-emit sit inside promote's `appendRefOps` closure, which `mutateShard` may re-invoke on a CAS retry, causing a possible counter over-count or duplicate manifest-trust event under contention; no correctness impact, same site/class as the older copy-forward increment.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/worklogs/2026-07-13-unattended-optimization-round.md#log"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1568", "topic": "production regression where a stateless test (`05008`) went red was traced to `U", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-156", "B029-002", "B032-041"], "canonical_claim": "A production regression where a stateless test (`05008`) went red was traced to `UniversePolicy::kDefault == StageA_Suppressed`, which suppresses destructive GC on every production round; the introducing commit swept 20+ gtest files and touched zero files under `tests/queries/`, so its stateless-test blast radius was invisible to its own test run.; Stage A sets `UniversePolicy::kDefault` to `StageA_Suppressed`, so every destructive GC site is suppressed by construction and no object is deleted during Stage A. Additionally, `UniversePolicy::kDefault` remains `StageA_Suppressed`; `AuthoritativeForTest` is the test-only policy value read by `Gc::fold` to set `universe_authoritative`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/cas/2026-07-28-stage-a-RESULTS.md#aggregate-posture", "docs/superpowers/cas/2026-08-02-stage-b-midpoint-audit.md#t7-universepolicy-confirmed-non-authoritative", "docs/superpowers/worklogs/CURRENT.md#L2219"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1569", "topic": "In the CA transient-classifier audit, `CasBlobInDegree", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-214"], "canonical_claim": "In the CA transient-classifier audit, `CasBlobInDegree.cpp:289` versus `pruneSupersededGenerations` (`CasGc.cpp:3217`, called from `:802`) has a plausible residual window where a deposed leader mid-read of a superseded generation could see benign absence misreported as `CORRUPTED_DATA`; it is GC-plane and contained, but worth confirming impossible.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-29-ca-transient-classifier-audit.md#L71-L73"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1570", "topic": "P3-B1 root cause was a pre-existing GC lease/fold livelock, not a Phase-3 regres", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-219"], "canonical_claim": "P3-B1 root cause was a pre-existing GC lease/fold livelock, not a Phase-3 regression: a large deletion pipeline makes each fold take minutes, the GC lease expires mid-fold, the other node legitimately adopts leadership, the first node's round CAS then aborts (`gc/state moved`) after its pre-CAS redeletes already executed, the pipeline never shrinks, and the cycle repeats as sustained dual-leader round fights; safety held throughout (dangling=0, unaccounted=0).", "issue_ids": ["P3-B1"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/reviews-todo.md#L123-L143"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1571", "topic": "C1(b) fix was not addressed as originally stated", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-222"], "canonical_claim": "The C1(b) fix was not addressed as originally stated: the frontier-completion predicate is byte-identical and the commit only changed the numerator's source; the equivalent guarantee holds only as an implicit invariant across three separate sites rather than the single explicit predicate that was requested, and this gap is exactly what NEW-1's incarnation-mismatch route exploits.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-30-stage-b-critical-checkpoint-review.md#L103-L107"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1572", "topic": "D4 (structural duplication)", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-232"], "canonical_claim": "D4 (structural duplication): delete-outcome classification (Deleted/NotFound/TokenMismatch/marker) is reimplemented with local accounting across GC's R3 phase, manifest cleanup, `cleanupRefObjects`, `runNamespaceCleanupPasses`, the orphan sweep, and the staging sweeper \u2014 same tolerated-outcome set, per-site counters/events, a semantic-drift risk though not yet a bug.", "issue_ids": ["D4"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L525"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1573", "topic": "D5 (structural duplication)", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-233"], "canonical_claim": "D5 (structural duplication): manual LIST/cursor pagination loops are independently implemented in GC fold, `changedShardCount`, the orphan sweep, fsck, `listMounts`, and `recoverRefTable`.", "issue_ids": ["D5"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L526"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1574", "topic": "D6", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-234"], "canonical_claim": "D6: GC (LIST `cas/refs/`), fsck (refs plus raw-listing diff), the orphan sweep (manifest paging plus watermark), and the frontend's `listNamespaces` (refs union roots) each discover the namespace/universe differently because each answers a different question (live set vs physical set vs eligibility); the definitions are not centrally owned, so fsck, GC, and the sweep can disagree \u2014 each grew independently in a different GC era.", "issue_ids": ["D6"], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L527"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1575", "topic": "Risk register #7 (Medium, confidence Med)", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-241"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #7 (Medium, confidence Med): `CasGc.cpp`'s `suppress_destructive` flag is computed twice from the same mutable `report.anomalies` (at `:635` and `:1149`), with consistency between the two computations implicit rather than enforced; a future edit could make fold suppress deletes while the round itself doesn't (or vice versa), an over-delete class. Suggested action: compute it once in `fold` and thread it through `FoldResult`.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L548"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1576", "topic": "Risk register #10 (Medium, confirmed)", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-244"], "canonical_claim": "Risk register #10 (Medium, confirmed): `ContentAddressedMetadataStorage.cpp:203,316` lazily creates `gc_scheduler` without a mutex; two concurrent `SYSTEM ... GC` statements could race the `make_unique` call, producing a torn scheduler pointer if not otherwise serialized upstream. Suggested action: confirm interpreter serialization, or add a mutex.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-12-cas-archaeology/00-REPORT.md#L551"]}
