{"cluster_id": "C-1580", "topic": "Blocking issue", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-345"], "canonical_claim": "Blocking issue: cursor pruning is not monotone across crash-resume \u2014 if the process stops after the pruned seal is durable but before `_ckpt`/entry deletion, the catalog still shows `Removing`, and a later GC round adds every `Live`/`Removing` entry back to `walk_targets` and writes a cursor for it, reintroducing the pruned cursor; the resumed removal driver then has no safe-and-live choice between trusting the historical pruned seal or waiting on the current seal indefinitely.", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-task5-critique-codex.md#blocking-1-cursor-pruning-is-not-monotone-across-crash-resume"]}
{"cluster_id": "C-1581", "topic": "rewritten Task 5's claim that stale-resume re-derivation is unconstructible cont", "target": "architecture/garbage-collection", "member_ids": ["AUDIT-M2-346"], "canonical_claim": "The rewritten Task 5's claim that stale-resume re-derivation is unconstructible contradicts both the spec's mandated capture-at-deposition rule and the existing TLA+ model `CaRefNsCleanupStaleLeaderCore.tla`, which constructs the exact interleaving (capture, stall, removal retires and deletes the entry, name is reborn, stale leader resumes and re-derives, targeting the live incarnation and violating `NoLiveDataDeleted`).", "issue_ids": [], "sources": ["docs/superpowers/reports/2026-07-31-task5-critique-codex.md#blocking-2-the-stale-resume-death-argument-contradicts-the-spec-and-an-existing-model"]}
